Legitimate Interests Assessment — security and abuse-prevention logs

Purpose test — legitimate interest: MIRA has a lawful, present, and specific interest in protecting user accounts/data, detecting technical failures, and preventing malicious or abusive use.

Necessity test: Limited event, error, and security logs may be necessary, but full questionnaire text, uploaded images, prompts, or generated outputs should not be copied into logs. Pseudonymous event IDs, redaction, role-based access, and a 30-day default retention are less intrusive means.

Balancing test: Users reasonably expect proportionate security logging, but not indefinite content logging or reuse. With transparent notice, minimisation, restricted access, automated deletion, and an objection process where applicable, the interest is unlikely to be overridden. Conclusion: Article 6(1)(f) is provisionally supportable; verify the actual logs before relying on it.

Legitimate Interests Assessment — product improvement

Purpose test: Improving MIRA is a lawful business interest.

Necessity test: Reusing identifiable questionnaires, photos, or inferred profiles is not shown to be necessary; synthetic, aggregated, or separately volunteered test data is a less intrusive alternative.

Balancing test: Users may not reasonably expect intimate self-description or images submitted for a one-off output to be reused for product development. Conclusion: legitimate interests are not established on the current facts; use separate consent or anonymised/synthetic data. Mark any alternative TBD — legal review.

Phase 4 — Risk and rights analysis

Special-category data — Article 9

MIRA does not require special-category data, but images and free text may reveal or lead the AI to infer ethnicity, health/disability, religion, political views, or sexual orientation. No Article 9 condition has been established, so the production design should prohibit questions and prompts targeting those traits, warn users not to submit them, filter outputs, strip unnecessary metadata, and delete accidental submissions. If deliberate processing is ever proposed, stop that feature pending legal review; explicit consent under Article 9(2)(a) cannot be treated as a casual checkbox and may be inappropriate where it is not genuinely necessary.

Automated decision-making — Article 22

MIRA performs automated scoring and profiling, but its creative recommendations do not have an intended legal or similarly significant effect. Article 22 is therefore unlikely to be triggered on the fixed fact pattern. The interface should nevertheless explain that results are AI-generated, present trait labels as suggestions rather than facts, permit correction/regeneration, and preserve the user’s and photographer’s authority to ignore every output.

