Two criteria clearly apply: evaluation/scoring and innovative technology. Under the EDPB screening approach, that generally indicates a DPIA is required. Complete it before public deployment, even though the prototype does not make significant decisions; include accidental sensitive-data inference, third-party images, vendor transfers, model reuse, security, and deletion cascades.

Data-subject-rights friction

Access (Article 15): Data may be spread across Tally, n8n execution histories, Sheets/Docs, AI-provider logs, Railway, and support systems. The current design does not evidence a single export; create a user/submission ID map and processor search procedure capable of producing inputs, inferences, outputs, recipients, purposes, retention, and safeguards within one month.

Erasure (Article 17) and withdrawal: Deleting a Google row alone would leave copies in executions, documents, AI/image jobs, logs, and backups. The current design does not evidence cascading deletion; implement a deletion workflow with vendor confirmations, backup expiry, exception handling, and an audit record.

Rectification (Article 16): AI trait labels may be inaccurate or reductive. Let users edit source answers, correct identity/contact data, reject labels, and regenerate outputs without retaining the superseded profile unnecessarily.

Restriction and objection (Articles 18 and 21): MIRA must be able to pause optional research and legitimate-interest processing while a request is assessed. A suppression flag is needed so erased/withdrawn data is not silently re-imported.

Portability (Article 20): For contract- or consent-based automated processing, provide user-supplied data in a structured, commonly used format such as JSON/CSV and offer generated outputs separately in standard formats.

Phase 5 — Law stacking check

AI Act cross-check: MIRA is not a high-risk Annex III system on the fixed facts because it does not govern access to employment, education, essential services, law enforcement, migration, or justice. It is best treated as a transparency-use case: clearly tell users they are interacting with AI, label AI-generated recommendations/images, and implement applicable machine-readable marking and deepfake/synthetic-content disclosures under Article 50 where the output meets those conditions.

ePrivacy check: The Tally/MIRA/Railway frontend and embedded vendor services must be scanned for cookies, pixels, local storage, and similar device access. Strictly necessary technologies may operate without optional consent where national implementation permits; analytics, advertising, cross-site tracking, or other non-essential technologies must remain off until valid consent is obtained. MIRA should not accept private-message screenshots or electronic-communication content without a separately assessed purpose and basis.

Data Act check: N/A on the current facts. MIRA does not obtain data from a connected product or IoT device and does not provide a data-processing service whose cloud-switching obligations are part of this MVP. Reassess if wearables, smart cameras, or connected-studio equipment are integrated.

Verify — accountability test

Using only the project documentation that existed before this lab, MIRA could not demonstrate full compliance to a regulator. This audit establishes the first data map and risk screening, but it does not prove vendor terms, transfer safeguards, consent, security, deletion, or rights handling. The missing evidence is itemised in mira_accountability_checklist.md.

